{"title":"Distinct Subsequences","difficulty":"Hard","statement":"You are given two strings `s` and `t`. A **subsequence** of `s` is obtained by deleting zero or more characters of `s` without changing the order of the rest. Two subsequences are counted as different when they use different sets of positions in `s`, even if they spell the same text.\n\nCount how many different subsequences of `s` are equal to `t`. In other words, in how many ways can the string `t` be found inside `s` by picking characters left to right? For example, if `s = aabb` and `t = ab`, the answer is 4: choose either `a` and either `b`.\n\nThe count can be enormous, so print it **modulo** `10^9 + 7`.\n\n**Follow-up:** interviewers often ask you to start from the plain recursion (take or skip the current character), state its time complexity, then add memoization and state the new complexity. Finally, can you reduce the memory to a single row?","inputFormat":"The first line contains the string `s`.\nThe second line contains the string `t`.","outputFormat":"Print a single integer: the number of subsequences of `s` equal to `t`, modulo 10^9 + 7.","constraints":["1 ≤ |s|, |t| ≤ 1000","s and t consist of lowercase English letters"],"examples":[{"input":"aabb\nab","output":"4","explanation":"Pick one of the two `a` and one of the two `b` that comes after it: 2 × 2 = 4 position pairs, each spelling `ab`."},{"input":"aacb\nab","output":"2","explanation":"There is one `b` (position 3) and two `a` before it, giving 2 ways."},{"input":"bbbb\nbb","output":"6","explanation":"Any two of the four positions work, and there are 4 choose 2 = 6 such pairs."}],"tests":[{"input":"a\na","output":"1"},{"input":"a\nb","output":"0"},{"input":"ab\nabc","output":"0"},{"input":"rabbbit\nrabbit","output":"3"},{"input":"abcabc\nabc","output":"4"},{"input":"abc\nabc","output":"1"},{"input":"aaaaaa\naaa","output":"20"},{"input":"cbabacbaaabcaaabacacccaa\nbbaaaa","output":"859"},{"input":"acbaabdbdcbcbaa\ncdbc","output":"4"}